/-! The flat-spectrum note on `x³ + x y mod p`, `review/flat_spectrum.md`, with the complex
readout `ψ x = exp (2πi x / p)`. Lemma 1: the nuclear norm is the least balanced
factorization cost `½ (‖U‖² + ‖V‖²)`, the weight-decay penalty of a bilinear layer, so
weight decay at its optimum is M: among the factorizations `U Vᴴ` of any width lying in a set
`S`, those of least weight decay multiply to exactly the matrices of least nuclear norm in `S`;
read through it, Theorems A and E below hold for weight decay at its optimum as well.
Proposition 2: `ψ (a + b)` has rank one and `‖K‖_S1 = p`; `ψ (a³ + a b)` has `K K* = p I`
and `‖K‖_S1 = p^{3/2}`; these are the extremes for unimodular entries, and relabeling keeps
the spectrum. Theorem A: an invertible `K` is the least-`‖·‖_S1` matrix agreeing with it on
`Ω` iff its polar factor vanishes off `Ω`; the polar factor of `ψ (a³ + a b)` is `K / √p`,
with no zero entry, so on no proper `Ω` is that kernel, or any square kernel with equal
singular values and no zero entry, even of least `‖·‖_S1` among the matrices equal to it
there. Corollary: with one cell missing,
the least-`‖·‖_S1` fill of a flat kernel (`K* K = N I`) is the opposite phase `−K_ij`, so for
`ψ (a³ + a b)` it decodes to `f + (p ± 1)/2` for odd `p` and never to `f`; the fill of the
rank-one `ψ (a + b)` is the truth. Theorem B: for prime `p` and `r ≠ 0`, the cubic sum
`S_r = ∑_x ψ(r x³)` has `|S_r| ≤ 2 √p`, and `S_r = 0` when `p ≡ 2 mod 3`; over uniform
`(a, b)`, `‖E[ψ_r(a b) | a³ + a b]‖²` is the source's exact expression in `S_r`, at most
`9/(2p) + 8/p^{3/2}`, so a readout of the label correlates with `ψ_r(a b)` by at most its
norm times `O(p^{-1/2})`. Each step of `v = a b`, `f = a³ + v` is rank 1 in its own basis,
`χ(a b) = χ(a) χ(b)` and `ψ_r(a³ + v) = ψ_r(a³) ψ_r(v)`, and flat in the other; the inner
products of the two bases are Gauss sums of modulus `√p`, and for `p ≡ 1 mod 3`,
`S_r = g(χ, ψ_r) + g(χ², ψ_r)` with `χ` of order 3. For a nontrivial multiplicative character
`χ` and `c ≠ 0`, Jacobi sums give Weil's bound `|∑_a χ(c − a³)| ≤ 2 √p`, so
`‖E[χ(a b) | a³ + a b]‖²` has the same bound `9/(2p) + 8/p^{3/2}`. Over `a ≠ 0`,
`1 + b/a² = f/a³`, and `E[χ(1 + b/a²) | f]` is `χ(f)` if `χ³ = 1` and `0` otherwise. For
nontrivial `χ` and odd `p`, the kernel `[χ(a³ + a b)]` has rank `(p − 1)/2` and
`r_eff = (√(p + 1) + (p − 3)/2 · √(2p))²/(p − 1)²`, between `47.9` and `48` at `p = 97`. For any
function `P` of `a`, the fibers of `P(a) + a b` give `‖E[ψ_r(a b) | f]‖²` exactly in terms of
`T = ∑_{a ≠ 0} ψ(−r P(a))`, and a bound on `∑_x ψ(r P(x))` or on `∑_a χ(c − P(a))` bounds the
additive or the multiplicative readout. For `P` of odd degree `m` and odd `p`, Weil's bound
(`Transformer.Weil`) gives `|∑_a χ₂(c − P(a))| ≤ (m − 1) √p`, so the Legendre readout
`‖E[χ₂(a b) | P(a) + a b]‖²` is at most `((m − 1) √p + 1)²/p² + (p − 1)²/(p² (2p − 1))`; for
even `m` at most one fiber fails, which adds `(p − 1)/p²`. The bound without that term holds for
every `χ ≠ 1` of order prime to `m`, by Weil's bound `|∑_a χ(c − P(a))| ≤ (m − 1) √p` for such
`χ`. For every `χ ≠ 1`, of any order, Weil's bound holds for `c − P` with an irreducible factor
`R`, `R² ∤ c − P`, which is every `c` but one: if all factors of `c − P` are squared, then
`c − P ∣ P'²`, and two such `c − P` are coprime, of total degree above `deg P'²` (for `P' = 0`
the trivial bound suffices). So the bound with the term `(p − 1)/p²` holds for every `χ ≠ 1`.
For a polynomial `P` of degree `m ≥ 1` prime to `p` and `r ≠ 0`, Weil's bound (`Transformer.Weil`)
gives `|∑_x ψ(r P(x))| ≤ (m − 1) √p`, so the additive readout `‖E[ψ_r(a b) | P(a) + a b]‖²` is
at most `((m − 1) √p + 1)²/p² + (p + (m − 1) √p + 1)²/(p² (2p − 1))`. Without a fixed
readout, for `f = P(a) + a b` with a polynomial `P` of degree `m ≥ 2`, every `ρ`, every `g` and
every centered `h` satisfy
`|∑_{a, b} ρ(f(a, b)) g(a) h(b)|² ≤ (1 + √((m − 1)(2p − 1))) ‖ρ‖² ‖g‖² ‖h‖²`. Every `P` has
`∑_a |∑_b ρ(f(a, b)) h(b)|² ≤ (p − 1) ‖ρ‖² ‖h‖²` for centered `h`, affine `P` reach it, and
once `(m − 1)(2p − 1) < (p − 2)²` it is reached iff `deg P ≤ 1`. Of the
numerical checks, the bound at `p = 97` is below `0.053`, `‖E[ψ_r(a + b) | (a + b)²]‖² = ½`, and the
counting rule `r (2p − r) ≤ ρ p²` iff `r ≤ (1 − √(1 − ρ)) p` gives `28.4` at `ρ = ½`, `p = 97`.
Torch check: every minimum-nuclear-norm completion is zero on a row whose seen entries are zero, so
a hidden 1 of the one-hot kernel `[a³ + a b = c]` in a row `a ≠ 0` is never recovered, from any
training set. The real readouts `cos(2πr f/p)` and `sin(2πr f/p)` of `f = a³ + a b` have rows `a`
and `−a` equal and opposite, so both are singular and Theorem A does not apply; for odd prime `p`
and `r ≠ 0` they have rank `(p + 1)/2` and `(p − 1)/2`, and every nonzero singular value is `√p`.
If every row of a one-hot kernel `[f = c]` holds at most one 1, its singular values are the `√N_b`
for the column counts `N_b`, so `r_eff = (∑_b √N_b)²/∑_b N_b`, with `∑_b N_b = p − 1` for
`a³ + a b` and `c ≠ 0`. For odd prime `p`, the indicator `[a³ + a b = 0]` of answer `0` has the
singular values `√(p + 1)` and `1` once each, `√2` `(p − 3)/2` times and `0` otherwise, so its rank
is `(p + 1)/2` and `r_eff = (√(p + 1) + 1 + (p − 3)/√2)²/(2p − 1)`, between `31.014` and `31.015`
at `p = 97` and between `59.532` and `59.533` at `p = 199`. The singular values of a kernel
`[g(a + b)]` are the `|ĝ(k)|` of the discrete Fourier transform. For odd prime `p`, the least
`r_eff` of `[φ((a + b)²)]` over the centered readouts, `∑_s φ(s²) = 0` with `φ` not zero on the
squares, is `2`, attained by `φ(s²) = cos(2π s/p)`; a constant readout gives `1`. For every
shift `μ`, the kernel `[a³ + a b = 0] − μ` has
`‖K_μ‖_S1 = (p − 3)/√2 + √(p + 2 − 2(2p − 1)μ + p²μ² + 2|1 − μ|√(p + 1))` and
`‖K_μ‖²_S2 = 2p − 1 − 2(2p − 1)μ + p²μ²`. Centered by its mean `μ = (2p − 1)/p²`, it has
`r_eff = (p(p − 3)/√2 + √(p³ − 2p² + 4p − 1 + 2(p − 1)²√(p + 1)))²/((2p − 1)(p − 1)²)`, between
`31.498` and `31.499` at `p = 97` and between `60.024` and `60.025` at `p = 199`, and `r_eff/p`
rounds to `0.359`, `0.345`, `0.325` and `0.302` at `p = 47, 61, 97, 199`. Exact recovery: if
`Y` vanishes off `Ω`, has unit row and column sums and `c (Y − J/N)` is a contraction for some
`c > 1`, and entries `r_i + s_j` vanishing on `Ω` vanish everywhere, then the all-ones `J` is
the unique least-`‖·‖_S1` matrix equal to it on `Ω`. When `τ σ⁻¹` is one `N`-cycle,
`(P_σ + P_τ)/2` is such a `Y` with `c = √(1 + 1/(4N²))`. For `N ≥ 2` the L2 operator norm
of `Y − J/N` is exactly `cos(π/N)`, reached by a character read along the cycle, so
`c (Y − J/N)` is a contraction exactly when `|c| cos(π/N) ≤ 1`; for `N ≥ 3` the best `c` is
`1/cos(π/N)`. Rescaling carries every recovery of `J` to every `a bᵀ` with
`|a i| = |b j| = 1`. So the `2p` values of `ψ(a + b)` on
two diagonals `b = a + s₁`, `b = a + s₂` with `s₂ − s₁` a unit determine it as the
least-`‖·‖_S1` completion, while on one diagonal that completion is not unique. The rigidity
hypothesis is necessary: if some `r_i + s_j` vanish on `Ω` but not everywhere, a second
rank-one matrix with entries of modulus one agrees with `a bᵀ` on `Ω`, so two diagonals
recover `ψ(a + b)` exactly when `s₂ − s₁` is a unit. It is not sufficient: row `i₀` and
column `j₀` are rigid and determine `a bᵀ` among rank-one matrices, yet the least nuclear norm
of the matrices equal to `a bᵀ` there is `2√(N − 1)`, below `N = ‖a bᵀ‖_S1` once `N ≥ 3`.
A missed block bounds the certificate: if `Ω` misses `R × C`, every such `Y` with
`c (Y − J/N)` a contraction has `c² #R #C ≤ (N − #R)(N − #C)`, so none exists with `c > 1`
once `R`, `C` are nonempty and `#R + #C ≥ N`, as when a row meets `Ω` in a single cell.
Without any certificate: if `Ω` contains the graph of a permutation, every matrix equal to `J`
on `Ω` has `‖X‖_S1 ≥ N`, and if `Ω` is also rigid, `J` is the unique minimum, as is every
`a bᵀ` with `|a i| = |b j| = 1`, `ψ(a + b)` among them. Without a permutation graph, Hall's
theorem gives rows `R` that miss a set `C` of columns with `#R + #C > N`, and
`J − s² 1_R 1_Cᵀ` is shorter than `J`. Exactly: for an `m × n` block `R × C` of size `k × l` and
every real `t`, `‖J − t 1_R 1_Cᵀ‖²_S1 = m n − 2t k l + t² k l + 2|t| √(k l (m − k)(n − l))`; for
nonempty `R`, `C` with `m n ≤ k n + l m` the least norm over `t` is `√(k (n − l)) + √(l (m − k))`,
and when `k n + l m ≤ m n` it is `√(m n) = ‖J‖_S1`, so some `t` beats `J` exactly when
`m n < k n + l m`, for `N × N` when `N < k + l`. Under the same conditions on `R` and `C`, the
least value over `t` is the least norm of all matrices equal to `J` off `R × C`: the matrix equal
to `1/√(k (n − l))` on `R × Cᶜ`, to `1/√(l (m − k))` on `Rᶜ × C` and zero elsewhere is a
contraction, so every matrix equal to 1 on `R × Cᶜ` and `Rᶜ × C` has
`‖X‖_S1 ≥ √(k (n − l)) + √(l (m − k))`. Hall's block can be taken with `k + l = N + 1`, and by
Cauchy–Schwarz every `Ω` without a permutation graph has a completion of `J` of norm at most
`√(N² − 1) < N − 1/(2N)`. For `k + l > N` the cells off a `k × l` block contain no permutation
graph and have least completion norm `√(k (N − l)) + √(l (N − k))`. With `k + l = N + 1` fixed
this value grows with `k l`, so for `a + b = N + 1` with `a ≤ b ≤ a + 1` the largest least
completion norm without a permutation graph is `√(a (a − 1)) + √(b (b − 1))`: `√(N² − 1)` for
odd `N`, `√2 + √6` for `N = 4`. By rescaling, the same bounds hold for every `u vᵀ` with entries
of modulus one, whose nuclear norm is `N`. So these kernels are recovered from `Ω` exactly when
`Ω` is rigid and contains the graph of a permutation. For any `a : m → ℂ` and `b : n → ℂ`, a
transport plan on `Ω` from `|a_i|²` to `|b_j|²` gives `‖X‖_S1 ≥ ∑ |a_i|² = ‖a bᵀ‖_S1` for every
`X` equal to `a bᵀ` on `Ω`, and if `Ω` is also rigid on the supports of `a` and `b`, `a bᵀ` is
the unique minimum: zero rows and columns need no cells. Conversely, `‖a bᵀ‖_S1 = ‖a‖ ‖b‖`;
without rigidity on the supports a second rank-one matrix of the same moduli agrees with
`a bᵀ` on `Ω`, and if rows `R` carry more than the columns `N(R)` they meet,
`‖a‖² ∑_{N(R)} |b_j|² < ‖b‖² ∑_R |a_i|²`, some completion is shorter than `a bᵀ`. From row
`i₀` and column `j₀`, `a bᵀ` is recovered exactly when `|a_{i₀}|²/‖a‖² + |b_{j₀}|²/‖b‖² ≥ 1`.
Gale's supply–demand theorem: nonnegative masses `α`, `β` have a plan on `Ω` exactly when
`∑ α = ∑ β` and every set of rows `R` has `∑_R α ≤ ∑_{N(R)} β`. So `a bᵀ` is recovered from
`Ω` exactly when `Ω` is rigid on the supports and every `R` has
`‖b‖² ∑_R |a_i|² ≤ ‖a‖² ∑_{N(R)} |b_j|²`; without zero entries, rigidity on the supports is
rigidity. If `|a_i| = 1` and `|b_j| = 1` on supports of the same size, Hall's theorem turns the
inequality into a bijection between the supports whose graph lies in `Ω`. A multiplicative
character `χ` of `ZMod p` is such a vector, vanishing only at `0`: `[χ(a b)]` is recovered
exactly when the cells with `a, b ≠ 0` are rigid on the nonzero residues and contain the graph
of a permutation of them, and `[χ(a + a b)] = χ ⊗ χ(1 + ·)` exactly when the cells with
`a ≠ 0`, `b ≠ −1` are rigid there and contain the graph of a bijection. Rigidity is
connectivity: with a row and a column, `Ω` is rigid exactly when the bipartite graph on the rows
and columns with an edge for each cell of `Ω` is connected, so a rigid pattern has at least
`#rows + #columns − 1` cells. Hence `a bᵀ` with `|a i| = |b j| = 1`, and so every rank-one
matrix with entries of modulus one, is recovered exactly when the cells of `Ω` connect the rows
with the columns and contain the graph of a permutation, and
`[χ(a b)]` exactly when the cells with `a, b ≠ 0` connect the nonzero rows and columns and
contain the graph of a permutation of them. The diagonal and the cells just right of it,
`2N − 1` cells, recover every `N × N` rank-one matrix with entries of modulus one, and no set of
fewer cells recovers one of them, so `2N − 1` is the least size of a set that recovers them all.
For `N ≥ 2`, two diagonals `b = a + s₁`, `b = a + s₂` of `ℤ/N` with `s₂ − s₁` a unit, minus any
one cell, are also such `2N − 1` cells: they connect the rows with the columns and keep one
diagonal whole, so they recover these matrices, `ψ(a + b)` among them.
For prime `p` and every multiplicative character `χ`, the `2p − 3` cells `(a, a)` and `(1, b)`,
`a, b ≠ 0`, recover `[χ(a b)]`, and no set of fewer cells does.
The readout decides: through `ψ_r`, `r ≠ 0`, the table of `a b` has equal singular values and
is of least `‖·‖_S1` on no proper `Ω`; some multiplicative character `χ` is injective, and
through it the table `χ χᵀ` of `a b` determines `a b`.
Read at `x = a/p`, `y = b/p` and divided by `p`, the table of `a b` is `frac(p x y)`: for
rational `α`, `frac(p α)` converges as `p → ∞` through the primes exactly when `2 α` is an
integer, by Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, and the points of the plane
where `2 x y` is an integer form a set of area zero.
At any rank, let `U`, `V` have orthonormal columns with `U Uᴴ K = K` and `tr((U Vᴴ)ᴴ K) = ‖K‖_S1`,
as a compact singular value decomposition of `K` gives. If `Y = U Vᴴ + W` vanishes off `Ω`,
